## Context

Child of tracker 0613 (goal 1), successor to 0642. `Ticket-ref: tickets/0613`,
`Ticket-ref: tickets/0642`, `Ticket-ref: tickets/0033` — none close.

**Why this ticket exists, precisely — read this before the mechanics.** Ticket
0642 landed durable, transaction-committed work counters on a branch of the
zoteus fork (`work-counters-0642`, `MinhHaDuong/zoteus`), proven correct: full
upstream test suite green (1097 tests), lint/build/typecheck clean, a live
build against a real 7,546-item library, durability proven by a forced-
rollback test. A draft upstream PR sits staged at
`verification/UPSTREAM-PR-WORK-COUNTERS-0642.md`, **not sent**.

The author's ruling (2026-09-04): passing tests proves the patch is
*correct*; it does not prove the patch *solves the problem it was built
for*. Running R22 against it did exactly that check, honestly: both
`R22-pause-stops-background-work` and `R22-pause-holds-across-restart`
**still report `not-run`**, because `bench/acceptance/durability.py`'s
`PERTURBATIONS` tuple has exactly one member, `EDIT_ONE_ITEM`, and zoteus's
adapter correctly refuses it (a write to the user's own Zotero library, R15-
excluded from derived state) — so `_the_change_creates_work`'s positive
control never fires for zoteus and both clauses die before grading. This
ticket's whole point is to close that gap and get a REAL local verdict —
**not** to write more code and call it done. The upstream PR stays unsent
until this ticket either proves it (a genuine PASS/FAIL, not a `not-run`
that quietly stayed) or reports honestly that it does not.

**Scope is entirely this repo's own harness — no more zoteus code needed.**
`durability.py`'s perturbation vocabulary and `zoteus.py`'s adapter are both
in `bench/acceptance/`, this repo. The perturbation itself is
`action:"build"` against a seeded, incompletely-embedded index — a verb
zoteus already has, wired through the adapter's own `perturb()` hook, never
a harness-side write to the target's storage (the same rule 0636's abandoned
sqlite-read design respected and this one must too).

**Respect the six review rounds already spent hardening `durability.py`'s
existing checks (ticket 0033's PR #255) — do not re-introduce a bug those
rounds already fixed.** Read `bench/acceptance/durability.py` and the R22
clauses in `bench/acceptance/assertions.py` in full before writing anything.
Load-bearing invariants from that history, all of which a new perturbation
must also satisfy: the positive control runs via a SEPARATE, never-stopped
target instance in its own arena (never a second use of the graded
instance — a shared root is refused, not measured); the restart clause's
graded window opens only after the restart settles (not across it); a
perturbation that cannot be driven is recorded against the verb that
failed, not silently folded into a false pass; `assess()` guards every
check call so a raised exception reaches the artifact as `not-run`, not a
crashed run with no artifact at all.

## Design

**The perturbation.** A new constant in `durability.PERTURBATIONS` — name it
for what it does, not for zoteus (`RESUME_EMBEDDING` or similar; the ticket
0636 sketched leaned this way too), since a build/embed-triggered background
job is a shape other targets could plausibly share later, even though only
zoteus implements it today. `zoteus.py::perturb()` currently refuses
`EDIT_ONE_ITEM`/`RESYNC_IDENTICAL_BYTES` (~lines 464-481, re-verify against
live source) — add a case that calls `action:"build"` against the seeded
fixture below.

**The fixture.** An index with `passagesWithoutVectors > 0` — a build
stopped mid-embed against a small fixture library. Confirm its shape by a
direct sqlite read (the adapter's existing `_index()`/`_stamp()` precedent
for reading, not writing) before trusting it in any check. This is real
work, not a stub: building it needs a real `fork/` (or the 0642 patch
branch checkout, since the fixture must be built against a binary that
reports the counter this ticket is trying to grade) and a real embed pass
long enough that `stop` can meaningfully interrupt it mid-flight — too fast
a fixture makes the perturbation race the embed pass to completion and the
test proves nothing.

**The checks.** `check_pause_stops_background_work` and
`check_pause_holds_across_restart` already read `durability.work_counters()`
and already run the positive-control-then-graded-phase machinery generically
— they should not need restructuring, only a target that can now supply
both a non-`None` counter object (0642) and a perturbation it accepts
(this ticket). If they DO need a change to accept the new perturbation kind
generically, make it generic (any target declaring support for this
perturbation), not zoteus-specific.

## Relevant files

- `bench/acceptance/durability.py` — `PERTURBATIONS`, `EDIT_ONE_ITEM`
  (~68-77), `work_counters()` (~112), `perturb()`'s docstring (~192, the
  "never reach past an adapter to write" rule).
- `bench/acceptance/assertions.py` — `check_pause_stops_background_work`
  (~779), `check_pause_holds_across_restart` (~853), `_pause_verdict`
  (~611-636), `_the_change_creates_work` (~639).
- `bench/acceptance/adapters/zoteus.py` — `perturb()` (~464-481, where the
  refusals live), `_index()`/`_stamp()` (the direct-read precedent),
  `seed_index` (the existing prebuilt-index-injection mechanism — the
  fixture below should reuse this, not invent a second seeding path).
- The 0642 patch branch (`work-counters-0642` on `MinhHaDuong/zoteus`) —
  this ticket's runs must build FROM this branch, not stock upstream, since
  the counter this ticket grades against does not exist on stock zoteus yet.
- `bench/results/0642-work-counters/` — the artifact this ticket's own
  result should sit beside, in the same ungraded-diagnostic shape (a real
  measured JSON + README, not yet the graded ladder verdict, since stock
  zoteus doesn't have the patch).
- `verification/UPSTREAM-PR-WORK-COUNTERS-0642.md` — the staged, unsent PR
  this ticket's result validates or does not; do not send it, whatever this
  ticket finds, without the author's own explicit go-ahead.

## Actions

1. Re-verify design against live source (both `upstream/main` for anything
   zoteus-side and the 0642 patch branch specifically for the counter's
   real shape) before writing code.
2. Build the fixture: seed an index with `passagesWithoutVectors > 0`
   against the 0642 patch branch, confirm via direct sqlite read.
3. Add the new perturbation constant and implement it in
   `zoteus.py::perturb()`.
4. Run `check_pause_stops_background_work` and
   `check_pause_holds_across_restart` against the patched, seeded build.
   `--posture account` should compose correctly (ticket 0637 fixed the
   podman-unshare ordering) — if it doesn't, that's a new finding to report,
   not something to route around.
5. Whatever the result — commit the artifact, honestly. If both clauses
   reach PASS or FAIL: that is the validation the author asked for, and this
   ticket's own log should say in as many words that the upstream PR is now
   confirmed to solve the goal-1 need, still pending the author's own send
   decision. If either clause still can't be graded, or grades in a way
   that reveals a further gap: report that plainly, do not force a green.
6. Append findings to 0613's, 0642's and 0033's logs (append-only).

## Test

Red-first, matching PR #255's own discipline: run the new checks against a
stub adapter that ignores `stop` (or reuses the existing
`stub-ignores-pause`/`stub-forgets-pause-on-restart` fixtures if they still
fit) and confirm FAIL, not `not-run` — a check reporting `not-run` against a
target that visibly kept working after `stop` was called is wired wrong. A
second positive control: the fixture's counter demonstrably CAN move (a
never-stopped second instance finishing the same fixture's embed pass)
before trusting that it did not move on the graded, stopped instance.

## Verification

- [ ] Fixture built and shape-confirmed by direct sqlite read
- [ ] New perturbation wired through `zoteus.py::perturb()`, no harness-side
      write to the target's storage
- [ ] Positive control demonstrated (counter moves on a never-stopped
      instance) before any graded result is trusted
- [ ] `check_pause_stops_background_work` and
      `check_pause_holds_across_restart` report PASS or FAIL (not `not-run`)
      against the patched, seeded build — artifact committed
- [ ] An explicit statement, in this ticket's own log, of whether the
      upstream counters PR is now validated as solving goal 1's need
- [ ] Upstream PR still NOT sent by this ticket, regardless of outcome

## Invariants

- No harness-side write to the target's storage, ever — the perturbation
  goes through `perturb()`/the target's own verbs.
- The existing `EDIT_ONE_ITEM`-based checks and their six-review-round
  hardening (independence guard, restart-window timing, guarded `assess()`)
  are not weakened or removed for other targets that DO support
  `EDIT_ONE_ITEM` — this adds a parallel path, it does not replace the
  existing one.
- The upstream PR is not sent by this ticket under any outcome; that
  decision belongs to the author.

## Exit criteria

Both R22 clauses report PASS or FAIL for zoteus against the 0642-patched
build, resting on a real run with a positive control, and this ticket's log
states plainly whether that constitutes the validation the author asked
for before the upstream PR can be considered for sending.
